## Independent local verification

Environment: clean isolated Git worktrees, external CPython **3.14.7** virtual environment,
Linux x86_64; PyYAML 6.0.3, jsonschema 4.26.0, NumPy 2.5.4, SciPy 1.18.1, SymPy 1.14.0,
mpmath 1.3.0. The [lock](../../tools/requirements-verification.lock) records exact transitive
package versions. Wheel hashes, operating system and hardware are not locked.

```sh
python tools/verify_research_baseline.py --root <clean-historical-worktree>   --expected-commit ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc
python tools/verify_prelaunch.py --full --base-ref origin/main
python tools/release_dependencies.py --check --summary
python tools/release_dependencies.py --local-candidate <new-unshared-directory> --check --summary
python tools/run_theorem_certificates.py --root <unshared-candidate>
git diff --check origin/main...HEAD
```

- Historical baseline: **7/7 stages**, **22 tooling tests**, **65 research tests**, and
  **94/94 registered certificates**; certificate phase **327.6 seconds**. All **19**
  documented file hashes and the recorded Git tree matched the exact historical commit.
- Final integrated code candidate: **13/13 stages**, including **81 tooling tests**,
  **17 catalogue/coordination tests**, **65 research tests**, and **94/94 certificates**.
  State, PR transaction, routes, entrypoints, catalogue roles, publication dependencies,
  documentation navigation and committed patch whitespace passed.
- Selective verification: real unregistered corner-helper change selected **94/94**;
  tests cover direct/transitive/dynamic/data paths, renamed/deleted files, newline
  filenames, doc-only skip, merge pushes, force pushes, missing ancestry, shallow and
  dirty trees, malformed events and live assertion/frontier errors.
- All **five** restored historical finite verifiers were separately rerun and passed.
  They were not newly registered or promoted. Their scripts state the encoded finite
  assumptions and omitted analytic/global conclusions.
- Offline onboarding from a clean checkout passed: **19 cards / 111 evidence references**,
  identical repeated seeded selection, and a valid six-part claim/handoff template.
  The live paginated read-only query worked; no issue/claim or collaborator invitation
  was posted.
- Actual unshared export passed generated-state, theorem-state and all internal-link/
  task-evidence checks, plus **94/94** registered certificates. Its selected artifact
  index had **634** records. Exact path counts can change with new infrastructure prose;
  use the current committed dependency plan, not a copied historical count.

Earlier failures were repaired: the raw combined README broke the old bullet-specific
route checker; a positive entrypoint fixture omitted linked directories; the first full
run passed all certificates but failed seven trailing-whitespace lines in one restored
report. The final run passes. Existing hosted failures had zero executed steps and a
billing/spending-limit annotation; they are not used as test evidence.

## Evidence restoration and release preparation

Dependency validation found missing source artifacts behind canonical audit links.
**17 historical artifacts (12 reports and five verifiers)** were restored from recorded
frozen commits. [Source commits and hashes](restored-evidence.json) preserve provenance;
only a recorded trailing-whitespace presentation normalization differs. Restored report
lifecycle headers remain unreviewed. The private artifact index grew from **1,410 to
1,422**; the theorem manifest remains **451 IDs / 845 edges / 94 certificates**. Two
preexisting relative references were corrected and the parser now excludes display
mathematics from Markdown link interpretation.

The dependency planner preserves essential proof/audit/task evidence, all research
runtime inputs where exact dependencies are uncertain, canonical definitions and the
complete ledger. It omits nonessential history and replaces uncleared bundled literature
with external references. The [release process](RELEASE_PREPARATION.md) validates an
actual local candidate; every selected file still requires exact-blob content, attribution,
privacy and rights review. Approval records stay private and outside the export to avoid
self-referential hashes and exposing reviewer records. A reviewed export is refused until
all file/owner/license gates pass. Structural QA never grants redistribution permission.

## CI and financial state

The [CI design](CI.md) uses one standard runner with read-only permissions and lightweight
integrity tests. Only explicitly reviewed workflow prose skips certificates. All research
code/data, canonical authority/proof inputs, verification infrastructure or uncertain
changes require the full suite. Main push ranges verify ancestry and both complete trees;
untrusted ranges fail closed. All 21 historical Target C workflows require owner dispatch
and an unset-by-default opt-in variable before any matrix expands. Comprehensive dispatch
also requires owner opt-in. No such dispatch or blocked-workflow retry was requested.

No paid usage, spending limit, plan upgrade or larger runner was enabled. Ordinary PR/main
events may create automatic hosted attempts, which are budget-blocked in this billing
cycle; inspect their zero-step results separately from local checks. At inspection main
had no branch protection or ruleset to bypass. Nothing changed those settings.

## Pilot and owner-controlled launch decisions

The private platform is prepared for a limited pilot by contributors who already have
authorized access. The owner still controls invitations and reviewer/merge permissions.
No pilot has been launched. The curated export is technically dependency-closed and
locally validated, **not cleared for public distribution**. The repository stays private.

Before public release the owner must:

1. Approve software, mathematical-text and contribution licensing terms after determining
   actual rights and contributor consent. Recommendation: review code and exposition
   separately; keep the default rights gate closed until explicit terms exist.
2. Approve each required blob's rights and attribution record. Recommendation: retain
   essential project evidence after review and use bibliographic links for papers/scans;
   do not infer consent from a task reference, audit or commit author.
3. Approve privacy, named attribution and redacted secret-scan clearance for the exact
   proposed tree. Recommendation: review the selected export, disclose no scan values,
   and avoid exporting the private historical Git database.
4. Choose a release destination/name and explicitly authorize publication. Recommendation:
   a separately approved clean-history curated repository; preserve this private archive.
5. Choose invited pilot contributors, independent mathematical reviewers and a small
   accountable merge group. Recommendation: use issue-based bounded claims and maintain
   the independent promotion gate; do not imply external peer review from internal audits.

These are outstanding human/owner decisions, not missing engineering implementations.
No license, release tag, repository visibility/ownership, collaborator permission or
publication action was chosen or changed during integration.

## Standalone export-validation repair

A final adversarial review found that standalone `--validate-export` needed the same
artifact-index/header recomputation already performed during candidate construction.
The focused [follow-up PR #975](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/975) adds that check: altered overlay index bytes and missing lifecycle
headers are rejected, while an intact generated export passes. This is an engineering
validation repair, with no mathematical state change; the follow-up verification record
is retained in its PR.

The follow-up complete local replay passed **13/13 stages**, **82 tooling tests**,
**17 catalogue/coordination tests**, **65 research tests**, and **94/94 registered
certificates**. An actual unshared 634-record export passed, rejected an intentionally
altered index, and passed after restoration. Validation also rejects an older export
when compared with a newer commit's file hashes; its correct pinned source ref passes.
The header-reader resource warning exposed by the new direct test was repaired.
# Publication hardening follow-up — 2026-10-15

This follow-up is engineering self-review with automated cross-checks. It supplies no
new mathematical audit, external peer review, consent or redistribution permission.
The fetched base was `4a10ce3b6e846f31b167b63aaa41abde9193b345`; theorem state remains
`2026-10-10.audit.234`. The manifest SHA-256 remains
`a8b571cacc2c77623a991f50114a70141d59bcde9685f916db8153599e58b2b6`.
Authority, route, theory, problem and active-prompt files are unchanged.

The integration retains the original commits of [#976](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/976),
[#977](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/977) and
[#978](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/978). The separate mathematical
audit [#979](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/979) was not integrated.

## Repairs and falsification

- Reject unsafe paths, symlinks and special files before reading exported content;
  check modes and source/overlay identities before writing into a private `0700` root.
- Bind Git reads to original objects despite local `git replace` references, verify
  commit/blob content hashes, and reject stale or duplicate approvals. Pending fields
  are listed without disclosing reviewer identities.
- Use matching scan/rewrite rules for code examples, display mathematics, comments,
  escaped/balanced destinations and references. Include raw HTML navigation; require
  Markdown syntax for bundled-literature rewrites. A separate CommonMark implementation
  exposed an unmatched-backtick bug during self-review; paragraph-boundary regressions
  now prevent it from hiding later evidence links.
- Remove a test's dependency on a bundled private PDF and an absent license. Its
  replacement checks exact committed inventories and a minimal clean-history fixture.
- Pin all 17 restored bodies and check their frozen sources optionally, without private
  history in the ordinary check. All 12 reports retain unreviewed lifecycle headers;
  their canonical audit records govern only the exact accepted statements.

## Recorded evidence and scope

The following complete code candidate was checked before the final Git-object guard:
`c6a99f8478536e5016d75e6b0ebdb4b787ab83f0`. Its full source replay passed **14/14 stages**,
including **104 tooling tests**, **18 catalogue/coordination tests**, **65 research tests**
and **94/94 certificates**, in **512.08 seconds**. Subsequent object/path guards have
focused regressions; the accompanying integration PR records the final reviewed head
and post-integration replay. Do not treat this earlier SHA as validation of later bytes.

At that candidate, two actual exports had identical **3,769 files and permissions**;
both passed validation, including standalone state/index/header checks. Their plan had
**9,333 dependency edges**, **19 cards**, **29 external citations** and **842 nonessential
excluded paths**. Construction/comparison took **193.33 seconds**. Counts belong to
that exact source commit; regenerate the plan for a later head.

All **13 mutation attacks** against an actual exported tree were rejected: altered
bytes, missing canonical audit and task evidence, corrupted state, stale views/index,
altered lifecycle header, executable mode, outside symlink, extra approval/PDF files,
traversal and wrong Git blob identity. The restored candidate passed again. Reviewed
export was refused before destination creation. Unit regressions additionally cover
special files, ambiguous/stale/partial approvals, binaries retained behind a blocking
gate, executable evidence, and Git replacement objects.

The five restored finite verifiers passed from the curated tree. All 19 task cards
validated there; repeated seeded output matched and the claim/handoff template was
complete. The separate clean-history newcomer copy passed **14/14 stages**, including
all **94 certificates**, in **433.09 seconds**. A read-only live query returned
catalogue-linked PR leads #947 and #953.
The independent CommonMark comparison found no omitted rendered local destinations
across **2,415 candidate Markdown files**, after accounting for the repository's math
extension. This is a corpus check, not general Markdown conformance.

Gitleaks **8.30.1**, downloaded from its official release with its published checksum
verified, reported **zero findings** in the restored candidate using default rules,
redacted output and no inline-allow suppression. A bounded privacy scan counted three
distinct email-shaped strings in seven files without disclosing values. Human privacy
and attribution review remain pending; scanner success is not clearance.

## Remaining release gates

| Gate | Condition and next action |
| --- | --- |
| Source/export mechanics | Local checks above pass at their stated SHAs; use the integration PR for final-head evidence and regenerate the exact plan. Validation assumes no concurrent writer. |
| Rights and license | Blocked: no root license, owner release approval or actual file approvals. Every retained blob still needs review. Use the [owner decision register](PUBLICATION_AUDIT.md#owner-decision-register-2026-10-15). |
| Privacy and attribution | Automated scan has no findings; human clearance and contributor consent remain unresolved. Keep actual reviewer records private. |
| Third-party material | No bundled PDFs/extractions in the candidate; quotations and citation metadata still require rights review. |
| Provenance | All 17 source hashes and normalized bodies match. Recovery and verifier passes do not promote their claims. |
| Hosted CI | Not executed: latest inspected job has zero steps and the payment/spending annotation. No retry, paid usage or historical matrix was enabled. |
| Governance | Owner must select maintainers/reviewers and future protection settings. Existing private `main` had no protection. No permissions changed. |
| Mathematical status | Level I/A/B/C-II remain open and C-I closed under the existing authority. External proof review was not established by this work. |

The main technical follow-up is a validated runtime-dependency graph if maintainers
want a smaller review set: the plan conservatively retains all research inputs. Never
remove them on filename or area/claim heuristics. Markdown extensions beyond the tested
syntax need regressions before becoming load-bearing navigation. Exact wheel hashes and
OS/hardware reproduction also remain outside the present version lock.

The next owner action is to decide the software, exposition and inbound-contribution
license policy, then commission exact-blob rights/attribution/privacy review. Publication
remains blocked. Keep this archive and every local candidate private.

# Overnight maintainer reconciliation — 2026-10-15

Starting main was `297d962c16cb89f73aa875640a7f5971e4048a8f`, state
`2026-10-10.audit.234`. This is engineering maintenance; Level I, A, B and C-II
remain open and C-I remains closed. No canonical theorem input was promoted or edited.

The complete paginated baseline contained 139 remote branches, 978 historical PRs,
21 open research PRs, four standalone issues, two tags and 119 registered worktrees.
A new durable private all-ref bundle was verified and a representative unmerged ref
was restored into a separate bare repository with a successful integrity check. All
29 dirty worktrees were separately archived and left in place.

Maintenance removed 75 stale remote refs with exact-SHA leases: 38 were ancestors of
main and 37 had their changed file blobs represented in main history. A separate
history pass inspected all 39 unique commits and 356 intermediate file versions in
that latter set. One intermediate audit-ledger note differed only by a superseded
promotion-transaction annotation; its original bytes remain in the private bundle.
No unique committed mathematical content was discarded. Twenty-two clean integrated worktrees,
33 absent-directory registrations and 35 integrated local aliases were removed.
Protected PR heads/bases and active/dirty worktrees were preserved. Ignored files in the
22 removed worktrees were not separately inventoried; their absence or preservation
cannot be certified retrospectively. Future removals require an explicit ignored-file
inventory as well as the ordinary status check. Gross deletions
and ending branch counts must be distinguished from new maintenance branches.

All 21 initial PRs received exact-head maintenance dispositions and isolated technical
reproduction: 21 theorem-state checks and 54 changed verifier/check scripts passed.
Nine merge-tree probes found textual conflicts. Eight intentional drafts remain drafts;
new substantive claims still require the mathematical review policy. The Seed 90
shared-label correction in #979 remains a specific dependency for repairing #972.
These are maintenance/reproducibility checks, not independent human proof reviews.
Historical issue closeouts point to their completed integrations; newly unique research
is retained for a separate preservation/review handoff.

- [#983](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/pull/983) adds the read-only paginated
  [branch audit/runbook](../MAINTENANCE.md) and exact-blob review grouping. No deletion
